  "variation": {
    "seeded": true,
    "notes": "Blade sizes, where the chips and leaves fly and the cut marks' shapes vary per cast. The three throws stay 110 ms apart and land on fixed frames (about 0.56, 0.67 and 0.78 s) so a game can hit three times on those frames."
  },
  "director_notes": "An original take on the wind slash, a staple of air magic. Choices: (1) a 0.32 s wind-up where pale streaks spiral into the hand and the first blade forms, so the volley reads as charged; (2) each blade is an orb with look crescent: a thin bow of air flying edge-first with a hard bright leading edge and fading after-images, because a slash needs a sharp, readable shape rather than a soft cloud; (3) three blades staggered by 110 ms, two curving in from opposite sides (side_offset_px) tilted opposite ways (tilt_deg) and one straight, so the volley reads as a fan in the top view and as crossing arcs in the side view; (4) each lands with a small local glint, chips, leaves and a dust puff, and a slash decal leaves crossing gouges; (5) sound: a high wind for the wind-up, three rising whooshes, and quick high cracks for the cuts. Blades are alpha-blended pale air with only a small HDR on the edge: wind cuts, it doesn't glow."
}
